Subject: [PATCH 0018] overlap Gauss-Seidel interface exchange with interior
 sweeps

F3 ordering change, authorised for the +/-0.5% final-Cd acceptance band.
This is not a bit-identical change when coupled cells are reordered. No
sweeps, equations, residual evaluations, component work or solver controls
are removed. The local arithmetic is still one source-plus-accumulator and
one division per cell, and two multiply/subtracts per internal face and
active component, plus the original interface contributions.

Cache coupled-cell membership through lduAddressing::patchAddr for every
set interface, including processor, cyclic and GAMG interfaces. Store
ascending contiguous runs, not a per-cell permutation in the hot loop.
The scalar smoother retains this cache alongside its 0011 accumulator;
its public static entry builds one cache per call. The batched component
solver builds one union cache per solve, covering all component interfaces.

For nonBlocking communications, initialise exchange, sweep the interior,
finish interface updates and then sweep the coupled cells. Preserve false
on init/update and the original scalar cmpt / component k, componentwise
boundary coefficients, request capture, tolerances and residual code.
Coupled psi entries stay unchanged until all relevant updates finish, so
processor send buffers and locally sampled cyclic neighbour values remain
sweep-entry values (the existing effective Jacobi coupling).

A plain cell-list permutation would break the 0011 forward scatter when an
interface owner has a numerically higher, interior neighbour. Cache rows
oriented from the earlier to the later endpoint in the new sweep order;
reverse such faces and swap upper/lower coefficients. An interior cell
then gathers the old interface value with the former lower coefficient,
and scatters its newly divided value with the former upper coefficient.
Boundary cells consume all earlier interior contributions, including those
with numerically higher labels. All scatter targets are still unvisited,
so resetting each consumed accumulator leaves every entry zero at sweep
and call boundaries. This is full local GS in the new permutation, with
the same two products per internal edge. Interface contributions can also
be reassociated with lower sums: coupled runs need F3 Cd validation.

Reuse original coefficient/address arrays if no edge reverses; otherwise
cache packed forward rows and coefficients once to avoid a per-face
coefficient-index indirection. This costs 20*nFaces + 4*(nCells+1) bytes
with DP/Int32, plus range storage, when reversal is needed. Matrix,
off-diagonal coefficients and interfaces must remain fixed for the cache
lifetime, as they do during these solves.

Component solvers share interface objects and their communication buffers:
never initialise multiple components concurrently. Instead split the
batched ascending interior sweep into balanced contiguous chunks, one
between each active component's init/update. Keep the three-component
kernel while all are active and the existing one-component fallback when
some converge. Sweep coupled cells only after all component updates.

With no coupled faces, use the original arrays and a single [0,nCells)
range AFTER all init/update calls, with no interior phase. The arithmetic
expressions and per-value operation order are unchanged from 034b3ffd;
uncoupled serial runs are bit-identical, including the 0011 accumulator
semantics. Buffered and scheduled communications also retain the original
ordering and arithmetic. All-interface cases have no useful overlap.

Affected paths: scalar smoothSolver (k/omega), scalar GAMG pressure levels,
batched vector components, static GaussSeidel calls and the GaussSeidel
member of DIC/DILUGaussSeidel. Other smoother implementations are unchanged.

Validation: GCC 13.3 -fsyntax-only passes for both modified C files, GAMG
solve, DIC/DILUGaussSeidel and simpleFoam (component templates instantiate
in smoothSolver.C). Standalone two-rank emulation passes 640 random
strictly diagonally-dominant symmetric/asymmetric scalar/component cases,
70 complete sweeps each: independent dense ordered-GS reference, unchanged
product counts, zero accumulators, stable interface send values and
zero-interface memcmp against extracted 034b3ffd row kernels. Both
orderings reach error <1e-12 within at most 16 sweeps in this test.
Native -O3 also passes, including direct baseline/current bit comparisons.
MPI overlap, performance and final-Cd +/-0.5% remain unmeasured locally.
AddressSanitizer/UBSan also passes all 640 standalone cases without findings.
